[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer**

*Use a **`color-relief`** layer.*  
It reads the same `raster-dem` source you already have and lets you tint the terrain on the fly with an `interpolate` expression over the special `["elevation"]` expression.

Below is a complete, ready-to-copy example that shows:

1. Adding the DEM source (if you haven’t already).  
2. Adding a `color-relief` layer that paints the DEM with a hypsometric ramp (blue → green → brown → white).  
3. Optional tweaks (opacity, exaggeration, sky, camera pitch) so you can see the effect.

---

## 1. DEM source (raster-dem)

```js
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // URL can be a TileJSON, a template string, or a PMTiles URL, etc.
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // If your DEM uses a non-default encoding (e.g. "terrarium") set it here:
  // encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12               // whatever your tiles support
});
```

> **Note** – The source is *only* a `raster-dem`. Do **not** add a `raster` layer for it; that would just display the raw RGB values of the DEM.

---

## 2. Hypsometric (color-relief) layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hypsometric-tint',
  type: 'color-relief',          // <-- the layer type you need
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  // No `source-layer` – raster-dem sources have no vector layers
  paint: {
    // The ramp – an interpolate expression over the special ["elevation"] value
    'color-relief-color': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['elevation'],               // elevation in metres (relative to the DEM's datum)

      // ---- elevation (m) ----   ---- colour ----
      -200, '#2e3b8f',   // deep water (optional, if your DEM includes bathymetry)
       0,   '#2c7bb6',   // sea level – deep blue
     200,   '#61a6d1',   // shallow water / low coast
     500,   '#a6d96a',   // low land – greenish
    1500,   '#ffffbf',   // mid-elevation – light yellow
    2500,   '#fdae61',   // higher – orange-brown
    3500,   '#d73027',   // sub-alpine – reddish
    4500,   '#ffffff'    // snow-capped peaks – white
    ],

    // Optional – make the tint semi-transparent so underlying imagery can be seen
    'color-relief-opacity': 0.85
  }
});
```

### How the ramp works

* `["elevation"]` is **only valid inside** the `color-relief-color` paint property.  
* The expression is evaluated for every pixel of the DEM tile, so the coloring happens entirely on the client.  
* The ramp can be as fine-grained as you like – add as many stops as you need to get the exact hue transitions you want.  
* The values are **meters above the DEM’s vertical datum** (usually sea level). If your DEM is encoded with an offset (e.g. Terrarium), the `encoding` you set on the source will be taken into account automatically.

---

## 3. (Optional) Show the terrain in 3-D so the tint is obvious

If you also want to see the surface extruded, add terrain and pitch the camera:

```js
// Enable 3-D terrain (uses the same DEM source)
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  exaggeration: 1.0   // increase >1.0 if you want more dramatic relief
});

// Add a sky so the horizon isn’t just a blank background
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#cceeff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});

// Tilt the view so the relief is visible
map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, bearing: 0, duration: 1500 });
```

> **Important:** The `color-relief` layer works **independently** of `setTerrain()`. You can keep the layer even if you later turn terrain off – it will simply render as a 2-D shaded raster.

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hypsometric Color-Relief</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [0, 45],
    zoom: 5,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // 2️⃣ Color-relief layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hypsometric-tint',
      type: 'color-relief',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'color-relief-color': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['elevation'],
          -200, '#2e3b8f',
           0,   '#2c7bb6',
         200,   '#61a6d1',
         500,   '#a6d96a',
        1500,   '#ffffbf',
        2500,   '#fdae61',
        3500,   '#d73027',
        4500,   '#ffffff'
        ],
        'color-relief-opacity': 0.85
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ (optional) 3-D terrain + sky + pitch
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#cceeff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });
    map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, duration: 1200 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 5. Quick checklist

| ✅ | What you need |
|---|----------------|
| **Layer type** | `type: "color-relief"` |
| **Source** | A `raster-dem` source (the same one you use for `setTerrain` if you have 3-D). |
| **Ramp** | `color-relief-color` → `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["elevation"], …]` |
| **Opacity** | `color-relief-opacity` (default 1). |
| **No pre-rendered tiles** | All coloring happens client-side; you don’t need a separate raster-color tileset. |
| **Works with terrain** | Yes – you can keep `map.setTerrain()` on or off; the layer is independent. |
| **Unsupported Mapbox-only props** | `raster-color`, `raster-value`, `raster-color-mix`, etc. – they are ignored in MapLibre. |

That’s it! With a `color-relief` layer you get a fully dynamic hypsometric map that updates instantly when you change the ramp, the opacity, or even the DEM source, without any extra server-side processing. Happy mapping!

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe for putting a pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap (JPEG/PNG tiles) behind all of your vector data and label layers in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1. What you need to know

| What you have | How MapLibre draws it |
|---------------|----------------------|
| **Pre-rendered relief tiles** (e.g. `https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg`) | **`raster` source + `raster` layer**.  The `hillshade` or `color-relief` layer types are *only* for DEM (`raster-dem`) sources, so they won’t work with a JPEG. |
| **Vector data & labels** (e.g. `fill`, `line`, `symbol` layers) | Already in your style.  Symbol layers (labels) are drawn on top of everything else unless you explicitly move them. |
| **Goal** | Add the raster layer **below** all other layers, but **above** the background.  Keep the vector layers (especially the label `symbol` layers) on top. |

---

## 2. Minimal style JSON (only the parts you have to add)

If you are building the style from scratch, you can insert the following objects into the `sources` and `layers` arrays of your style JSON.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    /* ────────────────────────────────────────
       1️⃣ Your existing vector sources go here
       ──────────────────────────────────────── */
    "relief-basemap": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": [
        "https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"
      ],
      "tileSize": 256,               // most raster tiles are 256 px
      "maxzoom": 14,                 // set to the max zoom your tiles support
      "attribution": "© My Relief Tiles"
    }
  },

  "layers": [
    /* ────────────────────────────────────────
       2️⃣ Insert the raster layer **first** (or before any other
           layers you want it to sit under).  The `before` key
           is optional if you add the layer programmatically.
       ──────────────────────────────────────── */
    {
      "id": "relief-raster",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "relief-basemap",
      "paint": {
        "raster-opacity": 1.0,          // 0-1 – tweak if you want the relief a bit translucent
        "raster-fade-duration": 0,      // no fade when you pan/zoom (optional)
        "raster-brightness-min": 0,     // keep the original JPEG brightness
        "raster-brightness-max": 1
      }
    },

    /* ────────────────────────────────────────
       3️⃣ Your existing vector layers (fills, lines, etc.)
           go **after** the raster layer.  If you already have
           a style file, just make sure the raster layer appears
           before the first vector layer you want it under.
       ──────────────────────────────────────── */
    /* example vector layer */
    {
      "id": "roads",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "my-vector-source",
      "source-layer": "roads",
      "paint": {
        "line-color": "#ffffff",
        "line-width": 2
      }
    },

    /* ────────────────────────────────────────
       4️⃣ Finally, your label (symbol) layers.
           Symbol layers are drawn in the order they appear,
           so keep them **after** everything else.
       ──────────────────────────────────────── */
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "my-vector-source",
      "source-layer": "places",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-size": 12,
        "text-font": ["Open Sans Regular", "Arial Unicode MS Regular"]
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#000000",
        "text-halo-color": "#ffffff",
        "text-halo-width": 1
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

### What the key fields do

| Field | Why it matters |
|-------|----------------|
| `type: "raster"` (source) | Tells MapLibre the tiles are images, not DEM data. |
| `tiles: [...]` | URL template for your JPEG tiles. |
| `tileSize` | Most raster tiles are 256 px; change to 512 if you serve 512-px tiles. |
| `maxzoom` | Prevents the map from requesting tiles that don’t exist. |
| `type: "raster"` (layer) | The layer that actually draws the source. |
| `raster-opacity` | Set < 1 if you want the relief to be semi-transparent so underlying vector fill colors can “show through”. |
| `raster-fade-duration: 0` | Removes the default 300 ms fade-in when you pan/zoom (optional but makes the raster feel snappier). |
| Layer order | The raster layer must be **before** any vector layers you want it under.  Symbol (label) layers should be the **last** ones. |

---

## 3. Adding the raster layer **programmatically** (if you already have a map instance)

If you prefer to keep your style JSON untouched and just inject the relief after the map loads, do this:

```javascript
// 1️⃣ Create the raster source
map.addSource('relief-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14,
  attribution: '© My Relief Tiles'
});

// 2️⃣ Add the raster layer *below* everything else
//    (use the id of the first layer you want it under, e.g. "roads")
map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'relief-raster',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'relief-basemap',
    paint: {
      'raster-opacity': 1,
      'raster-fade-duration': 0
    }
  },
  // <-- second argument is the layer id to insert *before*
  //     If you have a background layer you want it above, use that id.
  //     If you want it just under all vector data, pass the id of the
  //     first vector layer (e.g. "roads").
  'roads'   // <-- change to whatever your first vector layer id is
);

// 3️⃣ (Optional) Make sure label layers stay on top
//    If you ever add a new layer later, you can move it above the labels:
map.moveLayer('place-labels'); // moves it to the top of the stack
```

### Why the `before` argument matters

`map.addLayer(layerObject, beforeId)` inserts the new layer **just before** the layer whose id you pass.  
If you omit the second argument, the raster will be added on top of everything (covering your labels).  
So always give the id of the *first* vector layer you want the raster to sit **under**.

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Labels disappear** | Raster layer added *after* the label `symbol` layers (or without a `before` argument). | Re-order: `map.moveLayer('place-labels')` or add the raster with a `before` id. |
| **Tiles look blurry / pixelated** | Wrong `tileSize` (e.g. using 256 px source but telling MapLibre it’s 512). | Set `tileSize` to the actual size of your JPEG tiles. |
| **Map requests tiles at zoom levels you don’t have** | `maxzoom` missing or too high. | Add `maxzoom` (and optionally `minzoom`) to the source definition. |
| **Relief looks too dark or too bright** | JPEG already baked with contrast; you may want to tone it down. | Use `raster-brightness-min`, `raster-brightness-max`, or lower `raster-opacity`. |
| **Performance drops when panning** | Very high `raster-opacity` + many over-drawn vector layers. | Keep the raster as the *bottom* layer and avoid stacking multiple raster layers. |
| **Map shows a blank background where the raster should be** | URL template wrong, CORS blocked, or tiles not reachable. | Verify the tile URL works in a browser, and that the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). |

---

## 5. Full example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Relief basemap under vector data</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // 1️⃣ Your vector source (replace with your own)
        "my-vector-source": {
          type: "vector",
          url: "https://mytileserver.com/tileset.json"
        },
        // 2️⃣ Relief raster source
        "relief-basemap": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: [
            "https://mytileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 14,
          attribution: "© My Relief Tiles"
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // 3️⃣ Raster layer – placed before any vector layers
        {
          id: "relief-raster",
          type: "raster",
          source: "relief-basemap",
          paint: {
            "raster-opacity": 1,
            "raster-fade-duration": 0
          }
        },

        // 4️⃣ Example vector fill layer
        {
          id: "landuse",
          type: "fill",
          source: "my-vector-source",
          "source-layer": "landuse",
          paint: {
            "fill-color": "#e0e0e0",
            "fill-opacity": 0.6
          }
        },

        // 5️⃣ Example road line layer
        {
          id: "roads",
          type: "line",
          source: "my-vector-source",
          "source-layer": "roads",
          paint: {
            "line-color": "#ffffff",
            "line-width": 2
          }
        },

        // 6️⃣ Labels (symbol layer) – **must be last**
        {
          id: "place-labels",
          type: "symbol",
          source: "my-vector-source",
          "source-layer": "places",
          layout: {
            "text-field": ["get", "name"],
            "text-size": 12,
            "text-font": ["Open Sans Regular", "Arial Unicode MS Regular"]
          },
          paint: {
            "text-color": "#000000",
            "text-halo-color": "#ffffff",
            "text-halo-width": 1
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // Optional: add navigation controls
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.NavigationControl());
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the URLs (`mytileserver.com/...`) with the real endpoints for your raster relief tiles and your vector source.*

