## 6. TL;DR checklist

1. **Add a `raster

Below is a step-by-step recipe for putting a **pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap** (JPEG/PNG tiles) behind all of your vector data (roads, parks, labels, etc.) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1.  What you’re really adding

* A **raster source** (`type: "raster"`).  
* A **raster layer** (`type: "raster"`).  
* Nothing else – you don’t need a `raster-dem`, `hillshade`, or `color-relief` layer because the relief is already baked into the image tiles.

> **Why this works** – A raster layer is drawn exactly like any other layer in the style. Its only job is to copy the image pixels to the screen. If you put it early in the layer stack, everything that comes later (your vector layers, label symbols, etc.) will be drawn on top.

---

## 2.  Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief basemap + vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- your vector sources (e.g. OSM, custom tiles) ----
        "osm": {
          type: "vector",
          url: "https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/voyager/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // ---- a placeholder background (optional) ----
        {
          id: "bg",
          type: "background",
          paint: { "background-color": "#e0e0e0" }
        },

        // -------------------------------------------------
        // 2️⃣  Add the *shaded-relief* raster source & layer
        // -------------------------------------------------
        // (We add the source first, then the layer later – see step 3)
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Define the raster source (your JPEG tiles)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('shaded-relief', {
    type: 'raster',
    // Example URL pattern – replace with your own tile endpoint
    tiles: [
      'https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
    ],
    tileSize: 256,          // most JPEG/PNG tiles are 256 px
    // optional: maxzoom/minzoom if you know them
    // maxzoom: 14,
    // minzoom: 0,
    // optional: attribution
    attribution: '© My Shaded-Relief Tiles'
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Add the raster layer *before* any vector layers
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'relief-basemap',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'shaded-relief',
    // If you want the raster a little transparent so underlying background shows:
    // paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.95 }
  }, /* before layer id */ 'bg');   // <-- insert before the background (or any other layer you name)

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Add your vector data (roads, parks, labels, …)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Example: add a vector source + a line layer for roads
  map.addSource('roads', {
    type: 'vector',
    url: 'https://tiles.openstreetmap.org/roads/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'road-lines',
    type: 'line',
    source: 'roads',
    'source-layer': 'road',   // change to the actual source-layer name
    paint: {
      'line-color': '#ffffff',
      'line-width': 1.5
    }
  });

  // Example: add a symbol layer for place-name labels
  map.addSource('places', {
    type: 'vector',
    url: 'https://tiles.openstreetmap.org/places/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'place-labels',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'places',
    'source-layer': 'place',
    layout: {
      'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
      'text-size': 12,
      'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
    },
    paint: {
      'text-color': '#000000',
      'text-halo-color': '#ffffff',
      'text-halo-width': 1
    }
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  (Optional) Fine-tune layer order
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // If you ever need to move the basemap later:
  // map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', 'road-lines'); // puts it just below roads
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣** | Starts a map with any base style (you can even start from an empty style). | Gives you a canvas to work on. |
| **2️⃣** | Registers a **raster source** called `shaded-relief`. | MapLibre now knows where to fetch the JPEG tiles. |
| **3️⃣** | Adds a **raster layer** (`relief-basemap`) that draws the source. The second argument (`'bg'`) tells MapLibre to insert this layer *before* the background layer, i.e. at the very bottom of the stack. | Anything added **after** this layer (roads, labels, etc.) will appear on top. |
| **4️⃣** | Adds vector sources + layers (roads, labels). | Your data now sits above the shaded-relief image. |
| **5️⃣** | Shows how to re-order layers later if you need to. | Layer order is the only thing that determines “what’s on top”. |

---

## 3.  Quick checklist – does it work for you?

| ✅ | Item | What to verify |
|----|------|----------------|
| 1 | **Tile URL pattern** – `https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg` | Replace with your own domain, file extension, and any sub-domains (`{a-c}`) you use. |
| 2 | **Tile size** – `tileSize: 256` (or `512` if you serve 512 px tiles) | Wrong size makes the raster look blurry or mis-aligned. |
| 3 | **CORS** – The tile server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain) | Otherwise the browser will block the tiles. |
| 4 | **Layer order** – `relief-basemap` is *below* every vector layer you care about | Use `map.moveLayer()` or the `before` argument when you add a layer to guarantee ordering. |
| 5 | **Opacity (optional)** – `paint: { "raster-opacity": 0.9 }` if you want the raster a little translucent | Helpful when you have a background colour you want to see through. |
| 6 | **Attribution** – Add an `attribution` string to the source (or to the map’s `attributionControl`) | Keeps you compliant with the tile-provider’s license. |
| 7 | **Zoom range** – If your tiles only exist up to zoom 12, set `maxzoom: 12` on the source. | Prevents the map from requesting non-existent tiles (which would show as gray/blank). |

---

## 4.  Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Tiles appear as a solid colour or “missing”** | CORS header missing, or URL pattern wrong (404). | Open the tile URL directly in the browser → should show the JPEG. Add proper CORS headers on the server. |
| **Raster looks blurry at high zoom** | Tile size mismatch (`tileSize: 256` vs. 512) or `maxzoom` too low. | Set `tileSize` to the actual size of your tiles. If you only have tiles to zoom 12, set `maxzoom: 12`. |
| **Labels disappear under the raster** | Raster layer placed *after* the label layer. | Move the raster layer to the bottom: `map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', 'road-lines')` (or any layer that should be on top). |
| **Map background shows through the raster** | Raster opacity < 1 or the raster has transparent pixels. | Set `paint: { "raster-opacity": 1 }` or use fully-opaque JPEGs. |
| **Performance drops when panning** | Very large JPEGs (e.g., 1024 px) or many high-resolution zoom levels. | Stick to 256 px tiles, enable `tileSize: 256`, and consider using a CDN with HTTP/2 or Cloudflare caching. |
| **You want a hillshade effect *in addition* to the pre-rendered relief** | You can stack a `hillshade` layer on top of the raster, but it will double-shade and may look odd. | Usually unnecessary – the pre-rendered image already contains the shading you want. If you still need a subtle extra highlight, add a `hillshade` layer with `hillshade-method: "multidirectional"` and low `hillshade-exaggeration` (e.g., `0.2`). |

---

## 5.  Optional: Adding a **sky** or **fog** for a 3-D feel

If later you decide to enable true 3-D terrain (e.g., `map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem'})`) you’ll probably want a sky so the horizon isn’t just the page background. That is **independent** of the raster basemap you just added:

```js
// After you have a DEM source called "terrain-dem"
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.2 });

map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#88c0d0',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#c0d0ff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

*The sky does **not** affect your JPEG raster layer* – it only appears when the camera is pitched and terrain is on.

---

## 6.  TL;DR – Minimal code snippet

```js
// 1️⃣ Add raster source
map.addSource('relief', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
  tileSize: 256,
  attribution: '© My Shaded-Relief Tiles'
});

// 2️⃣ Add raster layer at the very bottom
map.addLayer({
  id: 'relief-basemap',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'relief',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 1 }
}, /* before */ 'background');   // or any other layer you know is near the bottom

// 3️⃣ Add your vector layers (roads, labels, etc.) *after* the raster layer
//    – they will automatically appear on top.
```

That’s it! Your pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap will now sit snugly beneath all of your vector data, with labels and other symbols rendered on top, just like a classic “raster-under-vector” map. Happy mapping!
